  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Typical summer weather tonight and Monday with near \naverage temperatures and late afternoon-evening showers/storms.\n\nNorthwest upper/mid level flow has set up over the forecast area as \nthe axis of a seasonably deep trough shifted offshore this morning. \nThis is typically a favorable set-up for severe weather across this \narea, and will likely be a factor in making up the the slightly \ncooler than normal temperatures acting to limit some instability, \nespecially today/tonight. The NW flow will work in tandem with a \nshortwave that is progged to move across the area this evening into \ntonight. This will help enhance the environment and bring good \nkinematics to help sustain storms when they develop. With the \nlowering heights, drier air aloft, and modest deep-layer shear, this \nevening could bring a mix of a wind threat and potentially a hail \nthreat. Hi-res guidance is depicting a late start to thunderstorm \ndevelopment, and this, paired with the best environment due to \nslightly higher dew points and better instability, will generally \nkeep the best chance for severe weather to the west of I-95 this \nevening into tonight. The farther east these storms progress, they \nwill enter a less favorable environment, but isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms are still possible across most of the area \nthrough tomorrow morning. \n\nAnother round of scattered storms is expected Monday, but with an \nearlier initiation time and coastal areas being included in the \naction. The NW flow will remain in place, and when combined with \nabout 30kt of bulk shear and 1500-2000 J/kg of CAPE, could help \nproduce stronger storms. Will note that some hi-res guidance has \nlesser coverage and is more focused on the sea-breeze initiation. \nThis will be dependent on the evolution of the storms through \ntonight and how the environment recovers, especially in the\npiedmont.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread showers and thunderstorms expected \nTuesday afternoon with the potential for damaging wind gusts and \nhail.\n\nThe set-up Tuesday continues to suggest that this will be the best \nshot at thunderstorms, with a decent chance for severe weather. SPC \ncurrently has a Slight Risk (2/5)  The Mid-Atlantic will still be \nunder NW flow as a cold front sweeps through at the surface. Global \nmodels continue to depict a strong upper trough becoming a cut off \nlow somewhere over the upper Mid-Atlantic or Northeast, but \ndifferences in the exact location of this low and subsequent surface \nboundary will impact timing. Regardless, confidence is increasing in \na strong synoptic set up for severe weather. Shear looks impressive \nby July standards, and the models mostly depict decent instability \nand even mid level lapse rates up to 6.0-6.5C, which would support a \nrobust damaging wind and hail threat. As always though, timing of \nthe storms will be important to how much severe weather occurs, \nthough there is good consensus that storm initiation will in the mid-\nlate afternoon. Highs Tuesday look to be in the upper 80s to around \n90F, along with humid conditions with dew points in low-mid 70s. \nStorms will be moving quickly, which will act to limit flooding, but \ncannot rule out localized flash-flooding, mainly in urban/flood \nprone areas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Some uncertainty regarding the forecast for \nWednesday, then mainly dry and near normal temperatures through the \nweekend.\n\nThere is some uncertainty in regards to rain chances in the wake of \nthe cold front on Wednesday. With the closer proximity of the upper \nlow, there cold be some showers on Wednesday. Blended guidance still \nhas low PoPs, but this may be adjusted depending on the location of \nthe upper low to our area. Thursday through the remainder of the \nweekend looks mostly dry, with seasonable temperatures.",
